<h2 id="ir-rules" style="color:#1565c0;font-size:1.22em;font-weight:650;border-left:4px solid #42a5f5;padding-left:10px;margin-top:1.1em">3. Related rules</h2>

| Rule | Use when |
|------|----------|
| **`.cursor/rules/mrkd-markdown-authoring.mdc`** | Normalizing or authoring the refactor plan **`.md`** itself. |
| **`.cursor/rules/exwar-war-stories-extraction.mdc`** (**exwar**) | After **each implemented phase**, deciding whether lessons qualify and **how** to append stories to **`war_stories/WAR_STORIES.md`** (metadata, **§N.1–N.5**, keywords, **`mrkd`** tables/Mermaid, collection outline/index updates). |

---

<h2 id="ir-s0" style="color:#1565c0;font-size:1.22em;font-weight:650;border-left:4px solid #42a5f5;padding-left:10px;margin-top:1.1em">4. Procedure: step 0 — normalize the plan Markdown</h2>

1. Open the refactor plan **`.md`**.
2. If structure is not yet **numbered phases + `[ ]` / `[X]` items**, **edit the file** to match [section 2](#ir-shape) and **MRKD** hierarchy (outline, at most three heading levels, consistent numbering).
3. Ensure every phase has **actionable** checkboxes; split vague prose into checkable items.

---

<h2 id="ir-s1" style="color:#1565c0;font-size:1.22em;font-weight:650;border-left:4px solid #42a5f5;padding-left:10px;margin-top:1.1em">5. Procedure: step 1 — ambiguity gate</h2>

1. Scan the plan for **product / schema / scope** decisions that **only the user** can answer (missing defaults, conflicting goals, breaking API contracts).
2. **If any remain:** reply with a **short numbered list** of questions only — do **not** start implementation. Stop.
3. **If none remain:** state “**No blocking ambiguities**” in one line and proceed to step 2.

---

<h2 id="ir-s2" style="color:#1565c0;font-size:1.22em;font-weight:650;border-left:4px solid #42a5f5;padding-left:10px;margin-top:1.1em">6. Procedure: step 2 — strengthen the plan (tests, comments, docs)</h2>

**Edit the same refactor plan `.md`** to add or reinforce two cross-cutting subsections (under the outline or as a dedicated **“Quality bar”** section):

| Area | Requirement |
|------|-------------|
| **Tests** | Each phase (or the plan global bar) must call for **appropriate** tests: **unit**, **integration**, **Playwright mocked <code>test:e2e</code>**, and/or **full-stack <code>e2e-test real</code>** where behavior crosses services or UI. Pick what fits; do not skip tests for risky paths. |
| **Comments and documentation** | **Sufficient informative comments** at important places (including a **brief file-level purpose** at top of touched modules where missing or stale). **Update** existing docs (README, <code>tests/README.md</code>, etc.) when behavior or commands change; add new doc only when the user or plan asks. |

After editing, proceed to step 3.

---

<h2 id="ir-s3" style="color:#1565c0;font-size:1.22em;font-weight:650;border-left:4px solid #42a5f5;padding-left:10px;margin-top:1.1em">7. Procedure: step 3 — implement the full plan</h2>

| Rule | Behavior |
|------|----------|
| **3.1 Stops** | Do **not** pause between phases to ask permission. Continue through **all** phases the user asked you to execute unless **blocked** — see **§3.1d** for the **closed list** of true blockers. If blocked, ask **precise, minimal** questions; after answers, continue. |
| **3.1b <code>resumimpl</code>** | Same as resume: pick up from first <code>[ ]</code> after user unblocks. |
| **3.1c Not a blocker (forbidden excuses)** | **Never** stop or defer **`implref`** solely because: a phase looks large or risky; the diff might be huge; migrations/UI feel “better as a separate PR”; you prefer confirmation before altering schema; subjective time estimates; internal model/context pressure **while the turn still runs** (**§3.1e** handles when the host **cuts** the turn); **skipping or deferring the [§3.4 exwar gate](#ir-s3-war)** to rush the next phase or because the user did not say `exwar`. **Instead:** execute the **next unchecked** actionable bullet (smallest increment that stays green under tests), and complete **§3.4** before marking a phase `[X]`. |
| **3.1d True blockers** | **Allowed** reasons to pause and engage the user **with the §3.1d label** in the closing table: build/test failures you cannot resolve in-repo; missing secrets/env; unresolved **ambiguous** requirements after **[step&nbsp;1](#ir-s1)**; irreconcilable spec vs code; destructive data change **without** rollback path required by the plan; **explicit** user revocation or smaller scope (**“only phases A–B today”**) where you **narrow** checklist targets **before** starting. |
| **3.1e Host / session cut** | If the tooling **truncates or ends** your turn (**output budget**, disconnect) before all **`[ ]`** → **`[X]`**, classify as **§3.1e Session cut** — **not** “implref succeeded.” Finish current edit batch safely; keep plan honest **`[ ]`**; **§3.5 → `wip`**; closing table **must** name **§3.1e** and the **exact next bullet + path** plus an explicit **`resumimpl`** follow-up naming that plan file. |
| **3.2 Plan updates** | After completing each **phase** (or major deliverable), set its bullets to <code>[X]</code> and mark the **phase heading** with <code>[X]</code> when fully done. Save the plan file in the same commit batch when practical. |
| **3.3 Cannot implement** | If something is **not** implementable for reasons **other** than 3.1 (e.g. out-of-repo service, scope cut), add a **new final phase** to the plan, e.g. **“Deferred / follow-ups”**, containing an **HTML table** (MRKD section 6.1 style: dark blue header row) with columns: **Item** · **Reason** · **Urgency** (brief) · **Recommended handling**. |

Immediately after each phase is implemented and **3.2** is updated for that phase, run [**step 3.4 — war stories**](#ir-s3-war) before starting the next phase.

---

<h2 id="ir-s3-war" style="color:#1565c0;font-size:1.22em;font-weight:650;border-left:4px solid #42a5f5;padding-left:10px;margin-top:1.1em">8. Procedure: step 3.4 — war story extraction after each phase (mandatory gate)</h2>

**Goal:** Capture **durable, learnable** lessons from **that phase**—not a per-`aciub`/`implref` receipt. This step is **blocking**: a phase is not done until exwar completes. Follow **`.cursor/rules/exwar-war-stories-extraction.mdc`** (**exwar**) end-to-end and **`.cursor/rules/aciub-allow-continue-iterations-until-blocked.mdc`** [§5 Exwar gate](#aciub-exwar-gate) when **`aciub`** is active.

**Default:** append a meaningful story unless the lesson is **already completely covered** in **`war_stories/WAR_STORIES.md`** or **`war_stories/WAR_STORIES_PROJECT_SPECIFIC.md`** (cite **#N**), or is **truly trivial** per exwar *What qualifies*.

1. **Read both collections** (indices + relevant bodies) before deciding skip vs append.
2. **Reflect** against **exwar** *What qualifies* and *Story kinds*. Prefer **mechanism** (trap, contract, design tradeoff) over “we shipped the checklist.”
3. **Skip only when exwar says so.** Thin polish, “did the ticket” with no insight, or **duplicate of an existing war-story index row** (cite story **N** — lesson **completely** already written) → **do not append**. In the [step 4](#ir-s4) summary, **one sentence** naming the skip reason (e.g. “skip: duplicate of story 83”; “skip: chrome-only, no reusable insight”).
4. **Do not skip because the pattern already exists in code.** “Mechanism already known from demos / another scope” or “similar helper already shipped” is **not** a valid skip — that is **code coverage**, not **lesson capture**. If this phase produced a clear insight and that insight is **not** already written in either `war_stories/` file, **append** it in the **same turn**.
5. **If it qualifies — choose the file** (see **exwar** routing):
   - **Portable / interview-transferable** → append to **`war_stories/WAR_STORIES.md`**
   - **Floor35 / this-repo product, routes, modes, pedagogy IA, repo-only ops harnesses** → append to **`war_stories/WAR_STORIES_PROJECT_SPECIFIC.md`**
6. When writing: metadata, **§N.1–N.5**, keywords, **`mrkd`** as needed; **update that file’s story index** in the same edit. **Absorb-in-place** (no canonical lesson only in `cursor_gen/` or chat).
7. **Do not inflate** thin lessons; **do not cut** a deep qualified lesson short only to stay brief.
8. **Tier A bias (not a force):** Plans with a Decision log / LM+schema arc often yield **one** design story—**unless** an existing index row already teaches the **same lesson text**. Never mint a story solely because a phase completed; never skip a design strategy lesson solely because a sibling scope already implemented a related pattern.

